          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag check did not match any emergency pattern. That is not a clearance. The check covers a fixed list, so if symptoms are severe, getting worse, or include anything new and unexplained, see a clinician promptly.\n\n**Background.** The sources describe chronic insomnia as trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, at least three nights a week for at least three months, despite having enough time to sleep, with daytime effects such as tiredness, poor concentration, or low mood (Insomnia summary; CBT-first-line guidance). Your three months of trouble may fit that description, but only a clinician can assess it. Whether daytime effects are present matters for that assessment.\n\n**Red flags.** My search for a specific list of sleep red flags did not complete, so I cannot list them from the sources. The one clear guidance point is that severe, worsening, or unexplained new symptoms call for prompt medical advice.\n\n**Possible causes to ask a clinician about.** The CBT guidance says clinicians commonly screen for:\n- Sleep apnea, where breathing repeatedly pauses or becomes shallow during sleep.\n- Restless legs syndrome.\n- Body clock (circadian rhythm) problems, including shift work.\n- Depression and anxiety.\n- Pain.\n- Effects of medicines or substances.\n- Caffeine, alcohol, and nicotine.\n\nThe same sources also link sleep trouble to generalized anxiety disorder and migraine. Iron deficiency can cause restless legs and fatigue. These are questions to raise with a clinician, not conclusions about you.\n\n**Next step.** Keeping a sleep diary for one to two weeks, noting bedtimes, wake times, caffeine, alcohol, and daytime tiredness, would give a clinician useful information.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
