          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is medication the usual answer?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: not usually, at least not first.** Across the guidance I found, medication is often one option among several, and it is frequently not the first step. The right first step depends on the condition.\n\n**Examples from the sources:**\n- **Chronic insomnia:** Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is first-line, ahead of medication. Medication is second-line and time-limited (Insomnia summary; CBT-first-line guidance).\n- **Generalized anxiety disorder:** Cognitive behavioral therapy and medication are both first-line options. They are chosen together with the person, not for them (Anxiety screening and first-line care guidance).\n- **High blood pressure:** For many adults with stage 1 hypertension and no other serious risk factors, lifestyle measures come first, with reassessment after three to six months before medication is considered (Hypertension first-line guidance).\n- **Type 2 diabetes:** Metformin, a medicine, is usually the starting drug alongside lifestyle changes when no other conditions complicate the picture. The choice can change if other conditions are present (Type 2 diabetes guidance).\n- **Heartburn (reflux):** An eight-week trial of an acid-reducing medicine is a common first step for typical symptoms without warning signs (Reflux guidance).\n\n**So what is your condition or symptom?** If you tell me which one you mean, I can explain what the sources say about its usual first-line approach. Please also note that I cannot tell you whether medication is right for you. That decision belongs with a clinician who knows your full history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
